Emacs does that, but it isn't actually all that useful; if you've written a
Lisp function that's long enough to need that, then you're probably doing
something wrong.

What is a lot more useful is the Meta-dot function, which warps you from the
invocation of a function to its definition. (Meta-comma takes you back)

Properly written Lisp functions have a tendency to flow towards south-east,
not south, so there is a built-in maximum length anyway.

